From: Daniel Borkmann <daniel@iogearbox.net>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 10:49:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bpf: Read security.bpf xattrs for LSM programs without the
 caller's permission

bpf_get_dentry_xattr() and bpf_get_file_xattr() check that the current
task may read the inode before they return an xattr, for user. and
security.bpf. names alike. For a BPF LSM program the labels it keeps
under security.bpf. are its own, and the task whose operation it decides
is the one the program may refuse: its read permission on the inode says
nothing about the label. The in-kernel LSMs read their labels with
__vfs_getxattr() in kernel context for that reason.

The check also lets that task choose what the program sees. A read that
fails with -EACCES or -EPERM looks to the program like a missing label
unless it fails every such operation closed, which it cannot do without
refusing every write-only open and every unlink of an unreadable file on
the system. Root arranges the failure for any labeled inode: an idmapped
bind mount shows uid 0's files as another id's to the permission check,
an ACL grants another id a write without a read, and a task at that id
through setfsuid() keeps CAP_SYS_ADMIN but not the DAC capabilities, so
the kernel lets it write what the program then takes for unlabeled.
Another LSM's inode_permission policy does the same.

Skip the permission check for security.bpf. names. The kfuncs are
registered for LSM and struct_ops programs, which run in kernel context
with the labels they maintain; user. names keep the check.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Borkmann <daniel@iogearbox.net>
[ spin-stack/machine: from shirudo's testing/kernel/patches, at
  versions.yaml's shirudo pin. ]
---
 fs/bpf_fs_kfuncs.c | 16 +++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fs/bpf_fs_kfuncs.c b/fs/bpf_fs_kfuncs.c
index 357a379..95c6abf 100644
--- a/fs/bpf_fs_kfuncs.c
+++ b/fs/bpf_fs_kfuncs.c
@@ -105,9 +105,19 @@ static int bpf_xattr_read_permission(const char *name, struct inode *inode)
 	if (!inode)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	/* Allow reading xattr with user. and security.bpf. prefix */
-	if (strncmp(name, XATTR_USER_PREFIX, XATTR_USER_PREFIX_LEN) &&
-	    !match_security_bpf_prefix(name))
+	/*
+	 * The labels a BPF LSM keeps under security.bpf. are its own, read
+	 * as SELinux, Smack and EVM read theirs with __vfs_getxattr(): in
+	 * kernel context, not with the permission of the task whose
+	 * operation the program decides. That task's read permission says
+	 * nothing about the label, and a read that fails for it, through an
+	 * idmapped mount, an ACL or another LSM's policy, would hide the
+	 * label from the program that enforces it. user. xattrs stay the
+	 * task's to read.
+	 */
+	if (match_security_bpf_prefix(name))
+		return 0;
+	if (strncmp(name, XATTR_USER_PREFIX, XATTR_USER_PREFIX_LEN))
 		return -EPERM;
 
 	return inode_permission(&nop_mnt_idmap, inode, MAY_READ);
-- 
